{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RURX6CnuRkHtnszf5CXAE8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\\label{sec:conclusion}\n2\t\n3\tWe evaluated SICA across seven models (7B--32B) on four reasoning domains ($N > 2{,}400$). Self-extracted constraints help only in a narrow Goldilocks zone ($\\sim$50--55\\% SC), with three failure modes outside it: confirmation bias at high accuracy (Type A, eight conditions, all n.s.), harmful noise at very low accuracy (Type C, $\\Delta = -4.67$\\,pp), and non-formalizable domains (Type B). Oracle experiments ($+14.22$\\,pp FOLIO, $+11.50$\\,pp ProofWriter) confirm aggregation works with discriminative constraints. Six remediation strategies, including ICL with gold FOL examples, all fail to widen this zone.\n4\t\n5\tFor deployment, $C_0$ and SC accuracy form a two-dimensional diagnostic: low $C_0$ renders SICA degenerate; high $C_0$ with high SC triggers Type A; high $C_0$ with very low SC risks Type C; only moderate SC ($\\sim$50--55\\%) is favorable, capturing 34.5\\% of the oracle ceiling. Trained extractors and external provers remain viable paths to close this gap."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018SZqD6yAwP96XPtA7TgLow","session_id":"6507131f-6f8a-40f6-a3e2-658e4faa24e8","uuid":"acc77e51-0762-46b0-9d02-c3fa4bdf2da8","timestamp":"2026-05-19T04:36:17.204Z"}
