{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WgUaCP6G1sd2t7HpnZoLLN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"SendMessage isn't available. I'll need to do the cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis myself based on the two reviews I've received. Let me proceed directly to Steps 2-4 and produce the final consolidated report.\n\nLet me synthesize the two reviews into a coherent roundtable outcome.\n\nBoth reviewers gave 3.5 (Borderline Conference) with confidence 4/5. Let me identify:\n\n**Convergence points:**\n1. Both agree oracle control is the strongest methodological contribution\n2. Both flag the Eff-K non-monotonicity (s42 vs s456) as undermining the Condorcet framing\n\n\n3. Both note the positive result rests on a single narrow condition\n4. Both find the nine failed remediations convincing\n5. Both score 3.5\n6. Both note variable naming canonicalization as an untested fix\n7. Both flag the constraint quality analysis being limited to Qwen (Type A) not Mistral (Goldilocks)\n\n**Divergence points:**\nR2 is more critical of the novelty claim, viewing the Condorcet framing as a straightforward application of existing theory, and raises specific technical concerns including missing citations (Lefort et al.), a mismatch between FOL and propositional logic, and questions about SICA's provenance. R1 takes a narrower focus overall.\n\nR1 zeros in on methodological issues like alpha sensitivity, extraction temperature, and per-problem agreement rates, while R2 identifies a fundamental tension between the paper's framing around independence and what the results actually demonstrate about extraction quality.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1607,"cache_read_input_tokens":70352,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1607},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","infere